# Cost Accounting and Indirect Cost Rate Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Section 1 — Purpose and Scope

This policy establishes the cost accounting framework that {{ org_name }} ("the Organization") uses to identify, classify, allocate, and charge costs to federal awards and other funding sources. It ensures that all costs meet the standards of the Uniform Guidance at 2 CFR 200 Subpart E and that the Organization maintains a consistent, auditable methodology for distinguishing direct costs from indirect (facilities and administration) costs.

This policy applies to all costs incurred by the Organization, regardless of funding source, including costs charged to federal awards, state grants, private foundations, and unrestricted funds. It applies to all directors, officers, employees, contractors, and consultants, and to the fiscal custodian.

## Section 2 — Cost Principles

Every cost charged to a federal award must satisfy all four of the following criteria (2 CFR 200.403-405):

1. **Allowable.** The cost must be permitted under the applicable cost principles (2 CFR 200.420-475), the terms of the federal award, and this policy.
2. **Allocable.** The cost must be incurred solely to advance work under the award, or it must benefit multiple objectives and be distributed in proportion to the relative benefit received (2 CFR 200.405).
3. **Reasonable.** A prudent person would have incurred the cost at the amount paid, given the circumstances at the time the decision was made (2 CFR 200.404).
4. **Consistently treated.** Costs incurred for the same purpose, in like circumstances, must be treated consistently as either direct or indirect costs. A cost may not be charged as a direct cost to a federal award if the same type of cost is included in the Organization's indirect cost pool.

## Section 3 — Direct Costs

Direct costs are those that can be identified specifically with a particular federal award, project, or other cost objective with a high degree of accuracy (2 CFR 200.413). The Organization treats the following as direct costs when they are incurred for and can be traced to a specific award:

- **Personnel compensation** — salaries, wages, and fringe benefits for individuals working directly on an award, supported by time-and-effort documentation per the Organization's Time and Effort Reporting Policy.
- **Supplies and materials** — consumable items purchased for use on a specific project.
- **Travel** — transportation, lodging, meals, and incidental expenses incurred for award-related travel, governed by the Organization's Travel Policy.
- **Equipment** — tangible personal property with a useful life of more than one year and a per-unit acquisition cost at or above the Organization's capitalization threshold ($10,000, or a lower threshold specified by the federal awarding agency or award terms), governed by the Organization's Property and Equipment Policy.
- **Subawards and consultant costs** — payments to subrecipients and independent contractors performing work under an award.
- **Other direct costs** — publication charges, participant support costs, specialized software licenses, and other costs incurred exclusively for a specific project.

When a direct cost benefits more than one award, it must be allocated among those awards using a reasonable and documented basis (e.g., proportional headcount, direct labor hours, square footage).

## Section 4 — Indirect Costs (Facilities and Administration)

Indirect costs — also called facilities and administration (F&A) costs — are costs incurred for common or joint objectives that cannot be readily identified with a specific project or award (2 CFR 200.56). Examples include:

- General administrative salaries (executive oversight, accounting, human resources)
- Office rent, utilities, and maintenance for shared spaces
- Organization-wide insurance, audit fees, and legal costs
- Depreciation on shared equipment and facilities
- Information technology infrastructure supporting multiple projects

### 4.1 De Minimis Indirect Cost Rate

The Organization has never had a federally negotiated indirect cost rate. Accordingly, it elects to charge the **de minimis rate of 15% of modified total direct costs (MTDC)** in accordance with 2 CFR 200.414(f), unless a sponsor or specific award imposes a lower permitted rate.

Modified total direct costs include all direct salaries and wages, applicable fringe benefits, materials and supplies, services, travel, and up to the first $50,000 of each subaward. MTDC excludes equipment, capital expenditures, charges for patient care, rental costs, tuition remission, scholarships and fellowships, participant support costs, and the portion of each subaward in excess of $50,000.

### 4.2 Path to a Negotiated Rate

When total federal funding grows to a level that justifies the administrative effort, the Organization will prepare an indirect cost rate proposal and negotiate a rate with its cognizant federal agency (the agency that provides the largest dollar amount of direct federal funding). The proposal will be prepared in accordance with 2 CFR 200.414 and Appendix IV to 2 CFR Part 200 (Indirect Cost Rate Proposals for Nonprofits). Until a negotiated rate is in place, the Organization will continue to use the 15% de minimis rate unless a sponsor caps recovery at a lower rate.

## Section 5 — Unallowable Costs

Certain costs are expressly unallowable under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.420-475) and may not be charged to federal awards, either as direct or indirect costs. The following is a partial list:

- **Alcoholic beverages** (200.423)
- **Entertainment costs** (200.438) — including amusement, social activities, and related costs
- **Fines, penalties, damages, and other settlements** (200.441)
- **Lobbying** (200.450) — costs of influencing federal or state legislation
- **Fundraising and investment management** (200.442)
- **Goods or services for personal use** (200.445)
- **Bad debts** (200.426)
- **Contributions and donations to others** (200.434)
- **Alumni activities** (200.421)

The fiscal custodian shall code unallowable costs to a designated account in the general ledger so they are excluded from all federal cost computations. When a cost is partially allowable, only the allowable portion may be charged to federal awards.

## Section 6 — Cost Allocation Methodology

The Organization uses the following approach to allocate costs across funding sources:

1. **Identify direct costs.** Charge each cost that can be specifically identified with a single award directly to that award.
2. **Allocate shared direct costs.** When a direct cost benefits multiple awards, allocate it using a documented, reasonable basis such as proportional labor hours, headcount, or units of output.
3. **Accumulate indirect costs.** Pool all remaining costs that support general operations into the indirect cost pool.
4. **Apply the indirect cost rate.** Apply the de minimis rate (or future negotiated rate) to MTDC to recover indirect costs from each award.
5. **Exclude unallowable costs.** Ensure that unallowable costs are excluded from both direct charges and the indirect cost pool before applying the rate.

The allocation methodology shall be documented and applied consistently across all awards and fiscal periods. Changes in methodology require written justification and Board approval.

## Section 7 — Fiscal Custodian Role

The fiscal custodian is responsible for:

- Maintaining the general ledger, chart of accounts, and project-level accounting records;
- Processing expenditures and ensuring proper coding to awards and cost categories;
- Preparing financial reports for sponsors, the Board, and external auditors;
- Tracking direct and indirect cost pools and computing indirect cost recovery;
- Segregating and flagging unallowable costs in the accounting system;
- Supporting the preparation of indirect cost rate proposals when the Organization transitions to a negotiated rate; and
- Retaining financial records in accordance with Section 9 of this policy.

## Section 8 — Single Audit Requirement

When the Organization expends **$1,000,000 or more in federal awards** during a fiscal year, it must undergo a Single Audit in accordance with 2 CFR 200 Subpart F (200.500-521). The Single Audit must be conducted by an independent auditor and must include:

- An audit of the Organization's financial statements;
- An examination of internal controls over compliance with federal award requirements; and
- A determination of whether the Organization complied with federal statutes, regulations, and award terms that could have a direct and material effect on each major program.

The audit must be completed and the reporting package submitted to the Federal Audit Clearinghouse within 30 calendar days after receipt of the auditor's report, or no later than 9 months after the end of the fiscal year, whichever is earlier. The fiscal custodian shall monitor aggregate federal expenditures throughout the year and notify the Board when the $1,000,000 threshold is approaching.

## Section 9 — Record Retention

All financial records related to federal awards — including general ledger entries, invoices, receipts, payroll records, allocation worksheets, indirect cost computations, and audit reports — shall be retained for a minimum of **three years** from the date of submission of the final expenditure report for the applicable federal award, in accordance with 2 CFR 200.334.

If any litigation, claim, audit, or negotiation involving the records begins before the end of the three-year period, the records shall be retained until the matter is fully resolved. Records shall be stored and managed in accordance with the Organization's Document Retention and Destruction Policy.

## Section 10 — Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually, typically at the Q1 board meeting, to ensure it remains consistent with current federal regulations and sponsor requirements. The date of each review shall be recorded in the board minutes.

